<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">War and march · 1919 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Wir sind des Geyers schwarzer Haufen</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A German marching song about the knight Florian Geyer, who fought on the peasants' side in the Peasants' War of 1525.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Heinrich von Reder</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Fritz Sotke (arranger)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the German youth movements of the Weimar Republic</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Germany</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>poem 1888; melody 1919</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>War and march</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words from the poem &quot;Ich bin der arme Konrad&quot; by Heinrich von Reder (1888); melody arranged by Fritz Sotke (1919))</dd></div></dl>
<p class="kd-note">This song is about hard things from the past, like war, cruelty or death. You can read about it with a grown-up.</p><button type="button" class="kd-story sg-kd-open">Read about this song</button>
<div class="sg-body sg-fold">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wir-sind-des-geyers-schwarzer-haufen">Wir sind des Geyers schwarzer Haufen</a>&quot; (&quot;We Are Geyer's Black Band&quot;) is a German marching song of the years between the world wars. Its words come from the poem &quot;Ich bin der arme Konrad&quot;, written in 1888 by the Bavarian poet and artillery officer Heinrich von Reder, and its melody was arranged by the songwriter Fritz Sotke in 1919.</p><p>The song looks back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-peasants-war">German Peasants' War</a> of 1525. It is named after Florian Geyer (1490 – 10 June 1525) and his Black Company, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> unit that fought on the peasants' side. In its own time the company was notorious for destroying cathedrals and castles and for summary executions of clergy and noblemen, and the song draws on that reputation; its words are noted for their strong hostility to the clergy and the nobility.</p><p>Composed within the German youth movements of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a>, the song was sung by groups across the whole political spectrum. It was later included in the official songbook of the National People's Army, the armed forces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">German Democratic Republic</a>. It remains popular with German music groups today, often with its words softened or with lines left out, depending on the arrangement and performer. A line of its last stanza is inscribed on a memorial at Osterhausen near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisleben">Eisleben</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a> that commemorates both the Peasants' War of 1525 and the land reform of 1945, which the memorial's designers saw as fulfilling the peasants' cause generations later.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A marching song in the voice of a peasant army of 1525, defiant and violent towards church and nobility, which looks ahead to later generations finishing their fight.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">German</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">German</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Wir sind des Geyers schwarze Haufen, heia oho!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We are Geyer's black bands, heia oho!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Und woll’n mit Tyrannen raufen, heia oho!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And want to brawl with tyrants, heia oho!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Spieß voran, drauf und dran,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Spears forward, at them and on,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">setzt aufs Klosterdach den roten Hahn.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>set the red cock on the monastery roof.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Wir wollen’s dem Herrn im Himmel klagen, kyrieleys,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We want to complain to the Lord in heaven, kyrieleys,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">daß wir den Pfaffen könnten totschlagen, kyrieleys.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>that we may not beat the priests to death, kyrieleys.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Spieß voran, …</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Spears forward, …</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Als Adam grub und Eva spann, kyrieleys,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>When Adam dug and Eve span, kyrieleys,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">wo war denn da der Edelmann? kyrieleys.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>where then was the nobleman? kyrieleys.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Spieß voran, …</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Spears forward, …</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Jetzt gilt es Schloß, Abtei und Stift, heia oho!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Now it is the turn of castle, abbey and chapter, heia oho!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">uns gilt nichts als die heil’ge Schrift, heia oho!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>nothing counts for us but the holy Scripture, heia oho!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Spieß voran, …</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Spears forward, …</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Uns führt der Florian Geyer an, trotz Acht und Bann.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Florian Geyer leads us, in spite of outlawry and excommunication.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Den Bundschuh führt er in der Fahn, hat Helm und Harnisch an.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He bears the Bundschuh on his banner, has helmet and armour on.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Spieß voran, …</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Spears forward, …</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Bei Weinsberg setzt es Brand und Stank, heia oho!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>At Weinsberg there was fire and stench, heia oho!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Gar mancher über die Klinge sprang, heia oho!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Many a man jumped over the blade, heia oho!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Spieß voran, …</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Spears forward, …</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Des Edelmannes Töchterlein, heia oho!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The nobleman's little daughter, heia oho!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">wir schickten es in die Höll’ hinein, heia oho!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>we sent her off into hell, heia oho!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Spieß voran, …</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Spears forward, …</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Geschlagen ziehen wir nach Haus, heia oho!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Beaten, we march home, heia oho!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">unsre Enkel fechten’s besser aus, heia oho!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>our grandchildren will fight it out better, heia oho!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Spieß voran, …</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Spears forward, …</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: after Heinrich von Reder (1888), as sung from 1919. Text from <a href="https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wir_sind_des_Geyers_schwarzer_Haufen" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (12)</summary><ul><li>'heia oho' and 'kyrieleys' are kept as sung: 'heia oho' is a wordless cry; 'kyrieleys' is a shortened 'Kyrie eleison' ('Lord, have mercy'), the refrain of old German pilgrims' and soldiers' songs.</li><li>'Spieß voran, drauf und dran' is translated plainly: 'Spieß' is the pike or spear, 'voran' forward, 'drauf und dran' onto them and at them. The source abbreviates the refrain after the first stanza ('Spieß voran, …'); kept so.</li><li>line 1.1: Florian Geyer (c. 1490–1525), a Franconian knight who led a peasant troop in the Peasants' War; 'Geyer' is kept as a name (it also means 'vulture'). 'Haufen' = a band or troop (the word for a fighting company of the time); the black band is his troop.</li><li>line 1.4: 'den roten Hahn aufs Dach setzen' (to put the red cock on the roof) is an idiom for setting a building on fire.</li><li>line 2.2: 'könnten' is read as a lament that they may not (are not allowed to) kill the priests; it can also be read 'that we could kill the priests'. 'Pfaffen' is a contemptuous word for clergy.</li><li>line 3.1-2: the old saying of the English Peasants' Revolt, 'When Adam delved and Eve span, who was then the gentleman?', in German.</li><li>line 4.1: 'Jetzt gilt es ...' = now it is aimed at, now it is the turn of; 'Stift' = a collegiate church or religious foundation (chapter).</li><li>line 5.1: 'Acht und Bann' = the imperial ban (outlawry) and the Church's ban (excommunication).</li><li>line 5.2: 'Bundschuh' = the laced peasant shoe, the emblem of the peasant risings, kept as a name.</li><li>line 6.1: Weinsberg, a town stormed in the Peasants' War; 'setzt es Brand und Stank' = there came (literally 'it set') fire and stench.</li><li>line 6.2: 'über die Klinge springen' (to jump over the blade) is an idiom for being put to the sword, killed.</li><li>line 7.2: 'in die Höll' hinein schicken' = to send into hell, i.e. to kill.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
